{"slug":"count-peaks","title":"Count the Peaks","topic":"arrays-strings","difficulty":"easy","order":112,"tags":["arrays","neighbours"],"timeLimitMs":2000,"concept":{"title":"Looking at neighbours","body":["Some questions compare an element with the elements next to it. The loop then runs over the **inner** positions, those that have a left and a right neighbour: `i` from `1` to `n - 2`.",{"code":"count = 0\nfor i in range(1, n - 1):\n    if a[i] > a[i - 1] and a[i] > a[i + 1]:\n        count += 1"},"Start the loop at 1 and stop before the last place, so that `a[i - 1]` and `a[i + 1]` always exist. For an array shorter than 3 the loop simply does not run."]},"statement":["A **peak** is an element that is strictly greater than both of its neighbours. The first and the last element have only one neighbour, so they are never peaks. Count the peaks of the array."],"inputFormat":["The first line has `n`.","The second line has `n` integers."],"outputFormat":["Print the number of peaks."],"constraints":["1 <= n <= 1000","-10^9 <= each number <= 10^9"],"examples":[{"input":"7\n1 3 2 2 5 4 6\n","output":"2\n","note":"The peaks are 3 and 5."}],"hints":["Only the positions 1 to n - 2 can be peaks.","Compare `a[i]` with `a[i - 1]` and with `a[i + 1]`.","The comparison is strict, so a plateau such as 2 2 is not a peak."],"starter":{"python":"n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\n# Your code here\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cin >> a[i];\n\n    // Your code here\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n        long[] a = new long[(int) n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) a[i] = sc.nextLong();\n\n        // Your code here\n    }\n}\n"},"solution":{"explanation":["Check every inner position against its two neighbours."],"complexity":"Time O(n), space O(1).","code":{"python":"n = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\ncount = 0\nfor i in range(1, n - 1):\n    if a[i] > a[i - 1] and a[i] > a[i + 1]:\n        count += 1\nprint(count)\n","cpp":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <string>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    long long n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) cin >> a[i];\n\n    int count = 0;\n    for (int i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        if (a[i] > a[i - 1] && a[i] > a[i + 1]) count++;\n    }\n    cout << count << \"\\n\";\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","java":"import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = sc.nextLong();\n        long[] a = new long[(int) n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) a[i] = sc.nextLong();\n\n        int count = 0;\n        for (int i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            if (a[i] > a[i - 1] && a[i] > a[i + 1]) count++;\n        }\n        System.out.println(count);\n    }\n}\n"}},"tests":[{"input":"7\n1 3 2 2 5 4 6\n","output":"2\n"},{"input":"1\n5\n","output":"0\n"},{"input":"2\n1 2\n","output":"0\n"},{"input":"3\n1 2 1\n","output":"1\n"},{"input":"3\n1 1 1\n","output":"0\n"},{"input":"5\n1 2 3 4 5\n","output":"0\n"},{"input":"6\n5 1 5 1 5 1\n","output":"2\n"},{"input":"25\n3 3 2 1 0 3 1 4 3 4 0 1 2 1 1 1 0 4 0 2 1 1 3 1 3\n","output":"7\n"},{"input":"1000\n-776594372 -450712145 647461773 515983182 -91180766 -382948920 -43826654 554826705 313045902 198725454 -779779226 381234197 -642787180 925482422 -923790935 244901675 619414908 143381620 -73732493 -632256243 264896467 989844292 -552242522 -211593250 66852844 576453092 -329169948 -395583418 78632347 -68110930 624619154 -687739562 321524161 227258037 -849191425 -726108490 -610292561 -843466456 -464823532 253492285 -98789